## 3. SOLID Violation Detection & Static Analysis Matrix

Static analysis tools can detect **structural symptoms** (high fan-out, circular imports, illegal cross-layer imports), but **cannot prove semantic SOLID compliance** (e.g., static analysis cannot know whether two methods serve the same business actor or whether a subtype subtly changes a domain invariant).

### Comprehensive Detection & Remediation Matrix

| Principle | Common Code Smell | Root Cause | Architectural Risk | Automated Detection Approach | Human Judgment Required | Recommended Refactoring | False-Positive Considerations |
| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |
| **SRP** | God Class / God Hook; High Efferent Coupling ($C_e > 10$); Mixing SQL, HTTP, and Business Math. | Feature accretion without domain refactoring; organizing code by technical layer instead of actor. | Merge conflicts across teams; slow/fragile unit tests; holding DB locks across network calls. | ESLint `max-lines-per-function`, `complexity`, `max-dependencies`; `dependency-cruiser` fan-out metrics. | Determining whether 5 methods serve *one* cohesive business actor vs. *multiple* independent actors. | Extract pure domain calculation functions; move external I/O to dedicated adapters; split post-commit side-effects into events. | Large generated parsers, state machine reducers, or configuration schemas may have high line counts while remaining cohesive to a single actor. |
| **OCP** | Repeated `switch (type)` or `if (provider === ...)` chains across multiple files; modifying core orchestrators for every new integration. | Lack of polymorphic strategy interface or plugin registry for volatile integration axes. | Regression risk in core tested flows; parallel feature branches colliding on the same file. | Git hotspot correlation analysis (`git log --format=format: --name-only` co-change frequency); AST lint rules flagging repeated union switches. | Distinguishing a **closed domain state machine** (where exhaustive `switch` is desirable) from an **open plugin axis**. | Introduce `Strategy` interface + NestJS multi-provider registry (`Symbol`) or React declarative plugin map. | Exhaustive `switch` over a fixed algebraic data type (`Result<T, E>` or `OrderStatus`) is an intentional best practice, not an OCP violation. |
| **LSP** | `throw new NotImplementedException()`; `if (repo instanceof SqlRepo)` checks in callers; divergent exception/null semantics. | Forcing partial adapters (e.g., read-only cache) to implement full read-write interfaces; loose contract documentation. | Runtime production crashes when swapping adapters; false confidence from unit tests using non-compliant fakes. | TypeScript `strictFunctionTypes: true`; ESLint `@typescript-eslint/no-unnecessary-condition`; banning `NotImplementedException` via custom AST rule; **Shared Contract Test Suites**. | Verifying semantic postconditions, idempotency, and error invariants across implementations. | Segregate read/write interfaces (ISP); replace inheritance with composition; enforce shared parameterized contract tests. | Abstract template methods designed to be overridden in subclasses may throw in base stubs if not marked `abstract`. |
| **ISP** | Unit tests littered with `{} as unknown as Service`; React components accepting 30-field DTOs to render 2 strings; bloated Context objects. | Passing convenience database entities or god services directly as parameter/prop types. | Brittle tests; broken `React.memo` optimizations; violating least-privilege security boundaries. | TypeScript compiler checking unused properties via custom lint rules; React Profiler detecting unnecessary Context re-renders; counting `as any` / `as unknown` in `*.spec.ts`. | Deciding the natural cohesion boundary of a role interface so it doesn't fragment into 1-method interfaces. | Use narrow Role Interfaces (`UserStatusReaderPort`), `Pick<T, K>`, or split React State and Dispatch contexts. | Passing a cohesive 4-field Value Object (`Money` or `Coordinate`) when a function only reads 2 fields is completely fine. |
| **DIP** | Domain/Application files importing `typeorm`, `@prisma/client`, `@aws-sdk/*`, `axios`, or `stripe`. | Writing code bottom-up from database tables or SDKs rather than top-down from domain use-cases. | Vendor lock-in across business logic; inability to run domain unit tests without infrastructure mocks. | **100% Automatable for Layer Imports:** `eslint-plugin-boundaries` or `dependency-cruiser` forbidding imports from `domain` $\rightarrow$ `infrastructure`. | Reviewing whether the domain port interface leaks low-level infrastructure semantics in its naming or structure. | Define Domain-owned Port interfaces + Symbols in `/domain/ports/`; implement Adapters in `/infrastructure/`. | Importing pure, zero-I/O utility libraries (`zod`, `decimal.js`, `date-fns`) inside domain code is safe and should be whitelisted. |

### Practical Static Analysis Configuration (`dependency-cruiser`)
Place the following rule in `.dependency-cruiser.js` to mechanically enforce **DIP** and **SRP/Modular Boundaries** in CI on every Pull Request:

```javascript
// .dependency-cruiser.js
module.exports = {
  forbidden: [
    {
      name: 'domain-must-not-depend-on-infrastructure-or-frameworks',
      severity: 'error',
      comment: 'DIP Enforcement: Domain layer must be pure TypeScript and own its ports.',
      from: { path: '^src/([^/]+)/domain/' },
      to: {
        path: [
          '^src/([^/]+)/infrastructure/',
          '^src/([^/]+)/application/',
          'node_modules/(typeorm|@prisma/client|@aws-sdk|axios|stripe|ioredis|nodemailer)',
        ],
      },
    },
    {
      name: 'application-must-not-import-concrete-infrastructure',
      severity: 'error',
      comment: 'DIP Enforcement: Application use-cases must depend on Domain Ports, not concrete Adapters.',
      from: { path: '^src/([^/]+)/application/' },
      to: { path: '^src/([^/]+)/infrastructure/' },
    },
    {
      name: 'no-circular-dependencies',
      severity: 'error',
      comment: 'Circular dependencies indicate entangled responsibilities (SRP/DIP violation).',
      from: {},
      to: { circular: true },
    },
  ],
};
```

