# The Ultimate Guide to SOLID Principles in React

Originally introduced by Robert C. Martin (Uncle Bob) for Object-Oriented Programming (OOP), the **SOLID** principles are just as vital in modern functional React. In React, we treat **Components** and **Custom Hooks** as our primary building blocks instead of classes.

Applying SOLID in React leads to codebases that are easy to test, refactor, scale, and reason about.

---

## 1. S — Single Responsibility Principle (SRP)

> **"A component or hook should have one, and only one, reason to change."**

In React, this means a single component shouldn't simultaneously handle data fetching, complex state management, data transformation, and UI rendering. If the API response changes, *or* the styling changes, *or* the business logic changes, that single component would have to be modified.

### ❌ Bad Example (Violating SRP)
Here, `UserList` does too much: it manages state, fetches data from an API, filters the data, and renders the UI.

```jsx
import { useState, useEffect } from 'react';

function UserList() {
  const [users, setUsers] = useState([]);
  const [loading, setLoading] = useState(true);

  // 1. Data Fetching Responsibility
  useEffect(() => {
    fetch('/api/users')
      .then((res) => res.json())
      .then((data) => setUsers(data))
      .finally(() => setLoading(false));
  }, []);

  if (loading) return <p>Loading...</p>;

  // 2. Business Logic / Filtering Responsibility
  const activeUsers = users.filter((user) => user.isActive);

  // 3. UI Rendering Responsibility
  return (
    <ul>
      {activeUsers.map((user) => (
        <li key={user.id}>{user.name} ({user.role})</li>
      ))}
    </ul>
  );
}
```

### ✅ Good Example (Following SRP)
We separate concerns: a **Custom Hook** handles fetching/filtering, a **Presentational Component** renders individual items, and the **Main Component** coordinates them.

```jsx
import { useState, useEffect } from 'react';

// 1. Hook responsible ONLY for fetching and preparing data
function useActiveUsers() {
  const [users, setUsers] = useState([]);
  const [loading, setLoading] = useState(true);

  useEffect(() => {
    fetch('/api/users')
      .then((res) => res.json())
      .then((data) => setUsers(data.filter((u) => u.isActive)))
      .finally(() => setLoading(false));
  }, []);

  return { users, loading };
}

// 2. Component responsible ONLY for how a single user looks
function UserItem({ name, role }) {
  return <li>{name} ({role})</li>;
}

// 3. Component responsible ONLY for assembling the list UI
function UserList() {
  const { users, loading } = useActiveUsers();

  if (loading) return <p>Loading...</p>;

  return (
    <ul>
      {users.map((user) => (
        <UserItem key={user.id} name={user.name} role={user.role} />
      ))}
    </ul>
  );
}
```

### 💡 SRP Rule of Thumb for React:
* If a component has multiple `useEffect` hooks doing unrelated things, extract them into custom hooks.
* Separate **Container/Smart logic** (hooks) from **Presentational/Dumb views** (JSX).

---

## 2. O — Open/Closed Principle (OCP)

> **"Software entities should be open for extension, but closed for modification."**

In React, you should be able to add new features or visual variations to a component **without editing its internal source code**. Instead of adding endless `if/else` or `switch` statements inside a component for every new requirement, use **Component Composition (`children` or render props)**.

### ❌ Bad Example (Violating OCP)
Every time a new page needs a different action in the `Header`, we have to open `Header.jsx` and modify it.

```jsx
function Header({ title, type }) {
  return (
    <header>
      <h1>{title}</h1>
      {type === 'dashboard' && <button>Create Report</button>}
      {type === 'profile' && <button>Edit Profile</button>}
      {type === 'settings' && <a href="/logout">Logout</a>}
    </header>
  );
}
```

### ✅ Good Example (Following OCP)
By using `children` (or slot props), `Header` is **closed for modification** (we never touch its code again), yet **open for extension** (we can pass anything inside it).

```jsx
// We never need to touch this component again!
function Header({ title, children }) {
  return (
    <header>
      <h1>{title}</h1>
      <div className="actions">{children}</div>
    </header>
  );
}

// Extended from the outside without modifying Header:
function DashboardPage() {
  return (
    <Header title="Dashboard">
      <button>Create Report</button>
    </Header>
  );
}

function ProfilePage() {
  return (
    <Header title="My Profile">
      <button>Edit Profile</button>
    </Header>
  );
}
```

### 💡 OCP Rule of Thumb for React:
* If you find yourself passing props like `isDashboard`, `showButton`, or `variantType` just to conditionally render different JSX blocks, replace them with `children` or specific slot props (e.g., `rightAction={<Button />}`).

---

## 3. L — Liskov Substitution Principle (LSP)

> **"Subtypes must be substitutable for their base types without altering the correctness of the program."**

While React rarely uses class inheritance, LSP applies heavily to **wrapper components** (like custom `<Input />`, `<Button />`, or `<Link />`). If you create a `SearchInput` that wraps a native HTML `<input>`, it should behave like an `<input>` and accept standard input props (`value`, `onChange`, `disabled`, `placeholder`, etc.).

### ❌ Bad Example (Violating LSP)
`CustomInput` claims to be an input wrapper, but it renames standard props and swallows native attributes like `disabled` or `placeholder`. Swapping `<input>` with `<CustomInput>` breaks expectations.

```jsx
function CustomInput({ text, handleTextChange }) {
  // Breaks standard <input> contract! What if I pass `placeholder` or `type="password"`?
  return (
    <input 
      className="border-2 p-2" 
      value={text} 
      onChange={handleTextChange} 
    />
  );
}
```

### ✅ Good Example (Following LSP)
By spreading the remaining native HTML attributes (`...props`), `CustomInput` fulfills the exact contract of a base `<input>` element while adding custom styles.

```jsx
function CustomInput({ className, ...props }) {
  return (
    <input 
      className={`border-2 p-2 ${className || ''}`} 
      {...props} // Forwards value, onChange, placeholder, disabled, type, etc.
    />
  );
}

// Usage: Works identically to a native <input>
function LoginForm() {
  return <CustomInput type="email" placeholder="Enter email" required />;
}
```

### 💡 LSP Rule of Thumb for React:
* When building Design System components (Buttons, Inputs, Modals), always accept and forward native HTML attributes using rest props (`...restProps`) and `ref` so the component never loses base functionality.

---

## 4. I — Interface Segregation Principle (ISP)

> **"Clients should not be forced to depend upon interfaces that they do not use."**

In React, a component's "interface" is its **Props**. Passing a massive object (like an entire database model) to a component that only needs one or two fields creates unnecessary coupling. It makes the component harder to reuse, harder to mock in unit tests, and prone to unnecessary re-renders.

### ❌ Bad Example (Violating ISP)
`UserAvatar` only needs an image URL, but it requires the entire `user` object. You cannot reuse `UserAvatar` for a "Company" or "Product" unless you fake a `user` object structure.

```jsx
function UserCard({ user }) {
  return (
    <div>
      {/* Passing the entire fat `user` object */}
      <UserAvatar user={user} />
      <p>{user.name}</p>
    </div>
  );
}

function UserAvatar({ user }) {
  // Depends on `user.profile.avatarUrl`, forcing tight coupling!
  return <img src={user.profile.avatarUrl} alt={user.name} />;
}
```

### ✅ Good Example (Following ISP)
Pass only the primitive props that the component actually needs.

```jsx
function UserCard({ user }) {
  return (
    <div>
      {/* Pass ONLY what UserAvatar needs */}
      <Avatar imageUrl={user.profile.avatarUrl} altText={user.name} />
      <p>{user.name}</p>
    </div>
  );
}

// Now completely decoupled from `user`—can be used for anything!
function Avatar({ imageUrl, altText }) {
  return <img src={imageUrl} alt={altText} />;
}
```

### 💡 ISP Rule of Thumb for React:
* Only pass the exact props a component needs.
* Prefer primitive props (`string`, `number`, `boolean`) over deeply nested object props for leaf/presentational components.

---

## 5. D — Dependency Inversion Principle (DIP)

> **"High-level modules should not depend on low-level modules. Both should depend on abstractions."**

In React, your UI component (high-level) should not directly depend on specific low-level implementations like `fetch`, `axios`, `localStorage`, or a specific state management library. Instead, it should depend on an **abstraction** (like a prop callback, a Custom Hook interface, or React Context).

### ❌ Bad Example (Violating DIP)
`LoginForm` is tightly coupled to `axios` and a specific API endpoint. You cannot test this form without mocking `axios` globally, and you cannot reuse this form in another part of the app that submits data differently.

```jsx
import axios from 'axios';

function LoginForm() {
  const handleSubmit = async (e) => {
    e.preventDefault();
    const email = e.target.email.value;
    // Tightly coupled to low-level implementation (axios + URL)
    await axios.post('https://api.example.com/login', { email });
  };

  return (
    <form onSubmit={handleSubmit}>
      <input name="email" type="email" />
      <button type="submit">Login</button>
    </form>
  );
}
```

### ✅ Good Example (Following DIP)
`LoginForm` depends on an abstraction (`onSubmit` prop). It doesn't care *how* the submission happens (Axios, Fetch, GraphQL, or a mock function in a test).

```jsx
// High-level UI component depends only on an abstraction (`onSubmit`)
function LoginForm({ onSubmit }) {
  const handleSubmit = (e) => {
    e.preventDefault();
    onSubmit(e.target.email.value);
  };

  return (
    <form onSubmit={handleSubmit}>
      <input name="email" type="email" />
      <button type="submit">Login</button>
    </form>
  );
}

// Low-level implementation injected from outside
function LoginPage() {
  const loginWithApi = async (email) => {
    await fetch('/api/login', { method: 'POST', body: JSON.stringify({ email }) });
  };

  return <LoginForm onSubmit={loginWithApi} />;
}
```

### 💡 DIP Rule of Thumb for React:
* UI Components shouldn't know *where* data comes from or *how* mutations are sent over the network.
* Pass functions via props, use Custom Hooks as façade interfaces, or inject services using `useContext`.

---

## ⚡ Quick Cheat Sheet Summary

| Principle | Core Concept | React Translation | Key Pattern |
| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |
| **S**ingle Responsibility | Do one thing well | Don't mix API calls, heavy logic, and UI in one file | **Custom Hooks** + Presentational Components |
| **O**pen / Closed | Extend without editing code | Stop adding `if/else` or `type` props to alter UI | **Component Composition** (`children` / slots) |
| **L**iskov Substitution | Subtypes act like base types | Custom `<Input />` or `<Button />` must act like native HTML elements | **Rest Props Forwarding** (`{...props}`) |
| **I**nterface Segregation | Don't pass unused data | Don't pass a huge `user` object if the component only needs `name` | **Destructured / Primitive Props** |
| **D**ependency Inversion | Depend on abstractions | UI shouldn't import `axios` or `localStorage` directly | **Callback Props**, Context, or Hook Abstractions |

